## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates a candidate's ability to model the economics of privacy-preserving data collaboration across financial institutions. It tests the capacity to balance data utility against privacy budgets while accounting for governance overhead and operational complexity.

## Key Concepts

- Differential privacy budgets and their impact on synthetic data fidelity
- Economic trade-offs in cross-organization data sharing
- Compliance, auditability, and governance cost modeling
- Phased rollout strategies with rollback and fallback mechanisms

## Code Example

```javascript
function computePrivacyBudget(epsilon, cohortSize, delta = 1e-5) {
  // Approximate (ε, δ)-DP budget allocation per cohort
  const epsPerCohort = epsilon / Math.sqrt(cohortSize);
  return { epsilon: epsPerCohort, delta, budgetExhausted: epsPerCohort < 0.01 };
}

function estimateFidelityLoss(privacySpend, baseFidelity) {
  // Fidelity degrades as more privacy budget is consumed
  return baseFidelity * Math.exp(-privacySpend);
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design privacy-preserving retention controls at the device edge, balancing storage, CPU, and regulatory costs while quantifying the business impact of data minimization.

## Key Concepts

- On-device policy engines and interpreters
- Data minimization and retention windows
- Batch policy delivery and anonymized audit deltas
- Compliance costs and telemetry design

## Code Example

```javascript
// Policy evaluator
function shouldDelete(event, policy) {
  const rule = policy.findRule(event.type);
  return event.timestamp < Date.now() - policy.getRetentionPeriod(rule);
}
```

